# Automation Testing Using Selenium Part - 3

<Section>
<IndexList>

## Concepts

<IndexItem href="1-registration-functionality">Registration Functionality</IndexItem>
<IndexItem href="2-converting-manual-steps-into-code">Converting Manual Steps into Code</IndexItem>
<IndexList>
</Section>

<Section>

## Introduction

In the previous unit, we learned about different locators in Selenium, such as ID, class, link text, partial link text, and XPath. We also explored various methods to perform actions on web elements, such as clicking buttons and entering text. We tested the login functionality of the NxtTrendz e-commerce Application.

In this unit, we will see more into selenium locators and explore more web interactions, including radio buttons, checkboxes, and dropdown.

</Section>

<Section>

## 1. Registration Functionality

In the previous unit, we tested the login functionality of the NxtTrendz e-commerce application.

In general, e-commerce applications offer a registration feature to allow new users to create accounts and access the platform's services. Therefore, users need to register on the website in order to obtain login credentials and access the site's functionalities.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-reg-form.png" alt="registration form">

So testing the registration functionality of the web page is important to ensure that new users can successfully create accounts and access the services provided by the e-commerce application.

Now, let's proceed with testing the functionality of the registration form.

In manual testing, we would typically fill in all the required details manually.

### 1.1 Testing Registration Functionality Manually

For registration, we may follow the below steps:

- Open Chrome browser.
- Enter the URL of the application in the browser.
- Fill in all the form fields.
- Click on the "Register" button.
- After successful registration, when the user clicks on the "login" link, they should be able to navigate to the login page.

By automating these steps, we can effectively test the registration functionality of the web application and ensure its proper functioning.

### 1.2 Automation Testing Registration Functionality

In automation testing, we will simply convert each step of the manual testing into code.

To convert the manual testing steps into automation code, we can follow three essential steps:

1. Project Creation
2. Convert Manual Steps into Code
3. Code Execution and Result Verification

**1.2.1 Project Creation**

We start the process of writing automation test cases by creating a project in the IntelliJ IDE.

#### Create Project

- Open IntelliJ IDE and click on the "Create New Project" button.
- Create the project with the following configurations

  - Project SDK: `OpenJDK 11`
  - GroupId: `org.example`
  - ArtifactId: `registration-test`
  - Project name: `RegistrationTest`
  - Project Location: `Choose the Project Location`
- Now, we create a `RegistrationTest` class in `src-->test-->java` directory.

#### File: `RegistrationTest.java`

```java
public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){

  }
}
```

We have successfully created the test class for our project. Now, let's proceed to execute the manual steps programmatically.

</Section>

<Section>

## 2. Converting Manual Steps into Code

Let's execute the below steps programmatically

- Open Chrome browser.
- Enter the URL of the application in the browser.
- Fill in all the form fields.
- Click on the "Register" button.
- After successful registration, when the user clicks on the "login" link, they should be able to navigate to the login page.


### 2.1 Open Chrome Browser

To integrate WebDriver into our project, we need to add a specific dependency in the **pom.xml** file.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-maven-reload.png" widthPercent="80" alt="dependency adding">

After adding new dependencies to a Maven project, we need to reload the project to reflect the changes.

We click on the **Reload** button to reload the Maven project.

We added the required dependencies for our project. Let's begin by writing our test case to validate the functionality of the registration page.

**2.1.1 Setup Chrome Driver**

To set up the Chrome driver, we will be writing the necessary code to configure and use it for our test automation.

```java
public class RegistrationTest {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
      // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
      System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");
  

  }
}

```

Here, we provide the path of the Chrome driver as a string parameter to the `setProperty()` method.

**2.1.2 Creating a WebDriver Instance**

To create a `WebDriver` instance and set up the Chrome driver, we can start by importing the necessary dependencies.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");
    
    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
    
  }
}
```
The `ChromeDriver` class implements the `WebDriver` interface and provides the necessary methods to interact with the Chrome browser

Let's execute the code and check the output.

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-chrome-instance-output.png" alt="chrome browser opened">

### 2.2 Navigate to the Registration Page

We use the `driver.get(URL)` method to navigate to the registration page.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

  }
}
```

Let's execute the above code and check the output.

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-open-reg-page-automation.png" alt="navigate to registration form">

### 2.3 Fill in all the form fields

In order to interact with different web elements on a web page, such as text fields, buttons, checkboxes, and links, it is necessary to first locate them.

To fill an input field, we first need to locate the web element in the DOM. Once the element is located, we can use Selenium locators to identify and interact with the element accordingly.

**Find Web Elements**

To locate an element in the HTML DOM of a web page, follow the below steps:

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-inspecting-gif.gif" alt="inspect">

- Right-click on the element.
- Select "Inspect" from the context menu.
- The browser's developer tools will open, highlighting the corresponding element in the DOM.
- Alternatively, we can also click on the "Select the element" button (usually represented by an icon like an arrow) and choose the element on the web page to locate it in the HTML DOM.
  <Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-inspection-selector.png" alt="using select the element button">

We know that there are various types of locators that can be used to uniquely identify a web element on a web page.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-locators-types.png" alt="different locators">

Now, let's locate the "name" element in the registration form and enter the desired name into it programmatically.

### 2.4 Find "Name" Web Element

Let's locate the "Name" element in the HTML DOM.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-name-in-reg.png" alt="find name element">

**Locating the "Name" Element on the web page**

We right-click on the "Name" element and select "Inspect" from the context menu.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-no-id-attribute.png" alt="Inspection name element">

Here we don’t have an id attribute, so we choose other locators like class name, tag name, name, etc.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-class-multiple.png" alt="same class name for different elements">

Here, we can observe that multiple elements on the web page share the same class name. However, to locate the specific "Name" element, we need to use a unique attribute that distinguishes it from the other elements.

We have the "name" attribute available for the element, which is unique in this case. Therefore, we can locate the element by its name attribute.

So, let's use the name attribute to locate the "Name" element.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-name-attribute.png" alt="name attribute">

Now let's write the code to locate the "Name" element in the registration form and enter the desired name.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

    // Find the username input element
    WebElement nameField = driver.findElement(By.name("name"));
    nameField.sendKeys("rahul");
  }
}
```

Here,

- We create a variable nameField of type WebElement to store the reference to the web element.
- The `name()` is a method of the `By` class used to locate elements by their "name" attribute.
- Within the parentheses of the `name()` method, we pass the attribute value we want to target. In our case, we use the string "name" as the attribute value.
- We use the `sendKeys("rahul")` method on the `nameField` element to simulate typing the text "rahul" into the name field.


Let's run the code to check the output.

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-name-output.png" alt="enter name">


Similarly, we locate the remaining elements of the registration form one by one using the id attribute.

