    WebElement genderField = driver.findElement(By.id("male"));
    genderField.click();
  }
}

```

Here, we use the `click()` method to select the "Male" option in the "Gender" section.

### 2.8 Find Country(Dropdown) Web element

Let's locate the "Country" element in the HTML DOM.

**Locating an Element on a web page**

We right-click on the "Country" element and select inspect.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-locate-country.png" alt="locating the country element">

Let's write the code to select an option in the country dropdown element.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

    // Find the username input element
    WebElement nameField = driver.findElement(By.name("name"));
    nameField.sendKeys("rahul");

    // Find the email input element
    WebElement emailField = driver.findElement(By.name("email"));
    emailField.sendKeys("rahul@example.com");
    // Find the password input element
    WebElement passwordField = driver.findElement(By.name("password"));
    passwordField.sendKeys("rahul@2021");

    // Find the male radio button
    WebElement genderField = driver.findElement(By.id("male"));
    genderField.click();

    // Find the country dropdown element
    WebElement countryDropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("country"));
    Select countrySelect = new Select(countryDropdown);

    // Select an option by visible text
    countrySelect.selectByVisibleText("India");
  }
}

```

Here, we selected "India" in the dropdown based on its visible text using the `selectByVisibleText()` method provided by the `Select` class. 

Let's run the code to check the output

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-dropdown.png" alt="dropdown output">

**Select Class**

In Selenium WebDriver, the `Select` class is used to interact with dropdown menus or select elements on a web page.

#### Syntax

```xml
Select element = new Select(webElement);
```
Here,

- The `Select` is the class name representing the dropdown select element in Selenium WebDriver. It is part of the `org.openqa.selenium.support.ui` package.
- The `new` is the keyword used to create a new instance of the `Select` class.
- `Select(webElement)` is the constructor of the `Select` class. It is called with the webElement object as an argument to create a new instance of the `Select` class.

The `Select` class provides various methods to interact with dropdown elements. Some of the commonly used methods are:

- `selectByVisibleText()`
- `selectByValue()`
- `selectByIndex()`

- The `selectByVisibleText()` method is commonly used when dealing with HTML `<select>` elements or used for selecting options from a dropdown menu. It allows us to choose an option based on its visible text

  It is important to note that this method is case-sensitive and requires an exact match with the text in the option.
- The `selectByValue()` method in the `Select` class is used to select an option from a dropdown. It allows us to select an element based on its value.
- The `selectByIndex()` method in the `Select` class is used to select an option from a dropdown. It allows select an element based on its index or order.

Let's consider a dropdown with the following HTML structure:

```html
<select id="dropdownId">
  <option value="1">Option 1</option>
  <option value="2">Option 2</option>
  <option value="3">Option 3</option>
</select>
```

Examples of using the `Select` class methods with the above dropdown:

#### 1. Select by visible text

```java
WebElement dropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("dropdownId"));

// Create a Select object
Select select = new Select(dropdown);
select.selectByVisibleText("Option 1");
```
Here, the `selectByVisibleText()` will select the option based on the text provided.

#### 2. Select by value

```java
WebElement dropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("dropdownId"));

// Create a Select object
Select select = new Select(dropdown);
select.selectByValue("2");
```
Here, the `selectByValue()` method will select based on the value provided in the "option" tag on the HTML page. In this case, it will select "Option 2".

#### 3. Select by Index

```java
WebElement dropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("dropdownId"));

// Create a Select object
Select select = new Select(dropdown);
select.selectByIndex(2);
```
Here, the `selectByIndex()` method selects an option based on its order/index. The index starts from 0, so in the provided example, it selects the "Option 3".

Now, we need to click on the check box to accept the terms and conditions.

### 2.9 Find Checkbox web element

Let's locate the "Checkbox" element in the HTML DOM.

**Locate the Check box element on the web page**

We right-click on the "Checkbox" element and select the inspect.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-locate-checkbox.png" alt="checkbox element">

Let's write the code to select the checkbox.

We know that to automate the process of clicking on web elements, we can utilize the `click()` method. 

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

    // Find the username input element
    WebElement nameField = driver.findElement(By.name("name"));
    nameField.sendKeys("rahul");

    // Find the email input element
    WebElement emailField = driver.findElement(By.name("email"));
    emailField.sendKeys("rahul@example.com");
    // Find the password input element
    WebElement passwordField = driver.findElement(By.name("password"));
    passwordField.sendKeys("rahul@2021");

    // Find the male radio button
