Here, we use the `click()` method to select the checkbox.

Let's run the code to check the output.

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-checkbox-output.png" alt="click on the checkbox">

Let's click on the register button to complete the registration.

### 2.10 Find the "Register" button web element

Let's locate the "Register" button element in the HTML DOM.

**Locate the "Register" button**

We right-click on the "Register" button element and select the inspect.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-register-locate.png" alt="locate the register button">

Here, the type attribute of an HTML element is set to "submit", indicating that it serves as a form submission button. To submit the registration form, we use the `submit()` method,

Let's write the code to submit the registration form.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

    // Find the username input element
    WebElement nameField = driver.findElement(By.name("name"));
    nameField.sendKeys("rahul");

    // Find the email input element
    WebElement emailField = driver.findElement(By.name("email"));
    emailField.sendKeys("rahul@example.com");
    // Find the password input element
    WebElement passwordField = driver.findElement(By.name("password"));
    passwordField.sendKeys("rahul@2021");

    // Find the male radio button
    WebElement genderField = driver.findElement(By.id("male"));
    genderField.click();

    // Find the country dropdown element
    WebElement countryDropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("country"));
    Select countrySelect = new Select(countryDropdown);

    // Select an option by visible text
    countrySelect.selectByVisibleText("India");

    // Select the checkbox
    WebElement termsAndConditions = driver.findElement(By.id("terms"));
    termsAndConditions.click();

    // Submit the registration form
    WebElement registerButton = driver.findElement(By.id("submitBtn") );
    registerButton.submit();
  }
}
```
Here, we used `submit()`  method to click on the register button.


Let's run the code to check the output.

#### Output

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-registration-successful.png" alt="registration successful">

Here, we the "Registration Successful" message.

After successful registration, when the user clicks on the "login" link, they should be able to navigate to the login page.


### 2.11 Find Anchor web element

Let's locate the login link and navigate to the login page.

**Locate the Anchor web element on the web page**

We right-click on the "Log in" hyperlink and select the inspect.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-6-locate-anchor.png" alt="locate anchor">

Let's write the code to click on the "Log in" hyperlink.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class RegistrationTest{
  public static void main(String[] args){
      
    // Set the path to the ChromeDriver executable
    System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

    // Launch the Chrome Browser
    WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

    // Open the NxtTrendz registration page
    driver.get("https://rahulnxttrends.ccbp.tech/");

    // Find the username input element
    WebElement nameField = driver.findElement(By.name("name"));
    nameField.sendKeys("rahul");

    // Find the email input element
    WebElement emailField = driver.findElement(By.name("email"));
    emailField.sendKeys("rahul@example.com");
    // Find the password input element
    WebElement passwordField = driver.findElement(By.name("password"));
    passwordField.sendKeys("rahul@2021");

    // Find the male radio button
    WebElement genderField = driver.findElement(By.id("male"));
    genderField.click();

    // Find the country dropdown element
    WebElement countryDropdown = driver.findElement(By.id("country"));
    Select countrySelect = new Select(countryDropdown);

    // Select an option by visible text
    countrySelect.selectByVisibleText("India");

    // Select the checkbox
    WebElement termsAndConditions = driver.findElement(By.id("terms"));
    termsAndConditions.click();

    // Submit the registration form
    WebElement registerButton = driver.findElement(By.id("submitBtn") );
    registerButton.submit();

    // Navigate to Login Page
    WebElement loginPageEl = driver.findElement(By.linkText("Log in"));
    loginPageEl.click();
  }
}
```

Here, we use the `linkText()` method to locate the "Log in" hyperlink.

The `linkText` is the locator strategy that targets an anchor tag <a> element based on its exact visible text content.

Let's verify the navigation to the login page after clicking the hyperlink.

```java
