## Introduction

In the previous units, we explored and tested a variety of web page functionalities using various locators, including ID, class name, tag name, link text, and partial link text.

In this unit, we will explore XPath and its various types. We will then apply XPath to test the login feature of NxtTrendz by locating the required elements.

## 1. XPath

XPath is the abbreviation for XML Path Language, which is commonly used to locate elements within the DOM (Document Object Model) tree.

XML Path Language (XPath) is a language that allows us to navigate and search for specific elements within an HTML and XML document.

Let's verify the login functionality of the NxtTrendz e-commerce application by utilizing XPath as the locator to locate the necessary elements.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-7-nxttrendz-home-page.png" alt="nxttrendz home page">

We need to find the username, password, and login button using XPath.

XPath in Selenium offers various approaches to find elements. Few commonly used approaches for locating elements using XPath::

1. Absolute XPath
2. Relative XPath
3. XPath Functions
4. XPath Axes


The Absolute XPath approach is useful when we need to locate elements based on their exact and specific path in the HTML structure.

Let's utilize the Absolute XPath approach to test the login functionality of the NxtTrendz e-commerce application.

We need to execute the following steps to test the login functionality.

- Launch Chrome Browser
- Navigate to the login page
- Enter the username and password
- Click on the login button
- User should be able to redirect to the Home page

### 1.1 Absolute Xpath

Absolute XPath is an approach that describes the exact location of an element within an XML or HTML document.

The absolute XPath is often referred to as Full XPath, which starts from the beginning of the HTML document.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-7-html.png" alt="html">

Let's consider an example of an XPath expression for an element in a sample HTML code.

Now, imagine we have a web page with the following HTML structure. Let's see how to locate the `<p>` element with "Grandchild1" as text content.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-7-sample-html.png" alt="sample HTML structure">

The absolute XPath expression for the "Grandchild1" element in the provided HTML code is `/html/body/div/div/p`.

The XPath expression `/html/body/div/div/p` selects the `<p>` element with the text "Grandchild1" that is nested inside two `<div>` elements, which are in turn nested inside the `<body>` element, which is inside the `<html>`(root element) .

This expression represents the complete path from the root node (/html) to the desired element.

Let’s find the username, password, and login button using absolute XPath.

