Great! In the current session, we have successfully tested the login functionality of the NxtTrendz website by utilizing the absolute XPath approach to locate the web elements.

But, despite being able to locate any element accurately in the HTML DOM tree using XPath, there is a disadvantage of using it.

**Disadvantage**

The disadvantage of the absolute XPath is that if there are any changes made in the path of the element then that XPath gets failed.

In our case, the absolute XPath of the password web element is `/html/body/div[1]/div/form/div[2]/input`.

Adding an element, like an email field, between the username and password fields would result in a modification of the absolute XPath for the password field.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-7-email-field.png" alt="adding email element">

Let's examine how the addition of the email field has modified the absolute XPath of the button element.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-7-email-div.png" alt="inspecting email">

Here, the Absolute XPath of the password web element is `/html/body/div[1]/div/form/div[3]/input`.

Previously, the button element was located in the second occurrence of the `<div>` element, but now it has changed to the third occurrence.

To address this problem, using relative XPath instead of absolute XPath is recommended.

Relative XPath expressions are more flexible, as they depend on the element's relationship to other elements rather than the entire path from the root.

In the next session, we will learn more about the Relative XPath.

</Section>

