### 1.1 Relative XPath

Relative XPath is an approach to locate elements within an XML or HTML document based on their relationship with other elements.

Relative XPath starts with a double slash `//` which means it can search anywhere in the DOM structure, while absolute XPath starts with a single slash `/` indicating that the path should start from the root node (`<html>`).

**Syntax**

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-8-rel-xpath-syntax.png" widthPercent="60" alt="relative xpath syntax">

Let's understand the syntax with the sample HTML DOM structure.

Imagine we have a web page with the following HTML Structure:

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-8-sample-html.png" widthPercent="50" alt="sample html">

The XPath syntax generally starts with a `//` double slash.

Let's see how to locate the `<p>` element with "Grandchild1" as text content.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-8-html-p-tags.png" widthPercent="50" alt="selecting element">

Using the XPath expression `//p` selects all `<p>` elements in the entire DOM tree, whether they are direct children of the top-level div or nested inside other elements.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-8-attribute-in-syntax.png" widthPercent="60" alt="attribute name in syntax">

In XPath, **@Attribute** represents the attribute name of a node.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-8-value-attrib-syntax.png" widthPercent="60" alt="value of attribute">

In XPath, the **Value of attribute** denotes the attribute value from the node.

**Relative XPath with class attribute**

When we try to locate an element using the `class` attribute of an element, we have to specify all the class names(in a space-separated manner) of the HTML element. 

For example, we have the element with HTML code as, 

```html
<div class="container pt-5 pb-5"></div>
```

To locate the element using the class attribute, we have to use the following XPath expression: `//div[@class='container pt-5 pb-5']`.

