## 2. XPath Functions

XPath functions are special functions that help us to perform different operations on elements based on certain conditions or criteria.

They allow us to perform more specific searches or operations on elements, such as finding elements based on their text content, attribute values, etc.

Selenium provides multiple XPath functions. Some of the most commonly used XPath functions to locate elements are as follows:

- text()
- contains()
- starts-with()
- position()

### 2.1 Using `text()` function

The `text()` function selects elements based on their text content. It is used to locate elements based on the text they display.

#### Syntax

```text
//tagname[text()='Text of the element']
```

Here,

- `tagname`: It is the tag name of the web element that we want to identify.
- `text()`: This method returns the text of the web element identified by the tagname, and it compares the value with the string provided on the right side.
- `Text of the element`: We write the full text of the element (in quotes) with which we want to identify the element.


**Example**

Imagine that we have a web page with the below HTML Structure:

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-9-sample-html.png" alt="sample html">

Let’s locate the paragraph element with the text "Child 2" using the XPath function.

The XPath expression of paragraph element with the text "Child 2" can be written as `//p[text()='Child 2']`. It identifies the web element that matches the specified text ("Child 2").

  - `//p` selects all `<p>` elements in the entire DOM tree.
  - The `text()` function returns the text of the `<p>` tag ("Child 2") and compares it with the text provided on the right side. It identifies the web element that matches the specified text.

We can see that all the products have unique titles. Therefore, we can locate the product using its title text.

