The `contains()` functions can also be used when the part of the attribute value changes dynamically. These functions can identify any attribute by using its partial value.

Let's look at the example to understand this.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-9-dynamic-id.png" alt="dynamic id">

In real-world applications, we may encounter scenarios where the part of the attribute value changes dynamically.

Here, we can use the `contains()` function to locate dynamically changing attributes by using a partial text that remains constant.

Let’s write an XPath expression to identify the `<p>` web element in the above example with an id attribute.

The relative Xpath expression is `//p[contains(@id, 'ild-')]`. We can use any partial text that is unique and remains constant for the specific element we want to locate.

Let's explore the following sample HTML structure and understand how to locate different elements using the remaining XPath functions.

### 2.3 Using `starts-with()` function

The `starts-with()` function is used when we want to locate an element by checking if the attribute value or display text starts with a specific substring.

#### Syntax

```text
//tagname[starts-with(@attribute, 'starting substring of value')]
```

Here,

- `@attribute`: Attribute of the Web Element
- `starting substring of value`: The starting substring of the value of the attribute/text.


Let's use the `starts-with()` function to locate the elements for the following sample HTML structure.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-9-1nxtwave.png" alt="sample html">

Let's locate the `<p>` element using the id attribute.

The XPath expression would be `//p[starts-with(@id, 'grand')]`. 

We can use any unique starting substrings of the attribute value to locate the element. In this example, we can also use substrings as 'gran', 'grandc', 'gra', etc

We can also use `text()` in place of an attribute. Let's locate the `<p>` element by using the substring "Gran" from the display text.

The XPath expression using the `text()` function would be `//p[starts-with(text(), 'Gran')]`.

**XPath functions with nested elements**

Some elements in the HTML DOM can be nested within other elements. We cannot identify such elements using the `text()` function, or other functions(like `starts-with()` and `contains()`) along with the `text()` function. For instance, if we want to locate the `<p>` element that contains other inline elements(like `<span>`, `<b>`, `<i>`, etc), we cannot use the `text()` function. We cannot use the functions `starts-with()` and `contains()` along with the `text()` function to locate the `<p>` element using the partial text in inline elements.

For example, consider the below HTML DOM, 

```html
<p class="description">
    Discover the power of <span>immersive interaction</span> in the virtual world with our cutting-edge technology.
</p>
```

Here, are some examples of valid XPath expressions:

✔ `//p[contains(text(), 'virtual world')]`
✔ `//p[starts-with(text(), 'Discover the')]`
✔ `//span[text()='immersive interaction']`

Here, are some examples of invalid XPath expressions:

✘ `//p[text() = 'Discover the power of immersive interaction in the virtual world with our cutting-edge technology.']`
✘ `//p[contains()='immersive interaction']`

### 2.4 Using `position()` function

The `position()` function in XPath is used to determine the position of an element within a set of selected elements.

#### Syntax

```text
//tagname[@attribute = 'value'][position() = n]
```

Here, **n** is the position of the element within a set of selected elements. We can also use the short form of the above XPath expressions syntax.

```text
//tagname[@attribute = 'value'][n]
```

Let's use the `position()` function to locate the elements for the following sample HTML structure.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-9-position-html.png" alt="sample html">

Let's locate the second `<p>` element within the `<div>` element that has a class attribute value of `parent`.

The XPath expression would be, `//p[@class="grandchild"][position()=2]`. The short form of the above expression will be `//p[@class="grandchild"][2]`.

<MultiLineNote>

The attribute values and display texts used in XPath expressions are case-sensitive

</MultiLineNote>

