## Introduction

In the previous unit, we learned how to use different locators to select web elements for testing various functionalities of a web application.

In this unit, we will learn about the CSS Selector locator and we will test the functionality of an application called Jobby app by selecting web elements using CSS Selector locators.

## 1. CSS Selector Locator

CSS Selectors are one of the locator strategies offered by Selenium to identify web elements.

CSS selectors primarily target elements based on their attributes, relationships with other elements, or specific element types. CSS selectors are similar to XPath with some advantages and disadvantages that we will discuss later.

Selenium offers different types of CSS selectors that are categorized as

- Simple Selectors
- Advanced Attribute Selectors
- Combinator Selectors
- Pseudo-class Selectors

Let's learn how to test some functionalities of the Jobby application using different types of CSS selectors.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-11-jobby-gif.gif" alt="jobby gif">

Let's understand how to use the Simple Selectors to locate the web elements.

### 1.1 Simple Selectors

Simple selectors are CSS selectors that target elements based on their basic properties such as
 
- Element type/tag
- ID
- Class
- Attribute

#### Syntax

```java
tagname[attribute = 'Value of attribute']
```

Here, 

- `tagname`: It is the tag name of the web element that we want to identify
- `attribute`: Name of the attribute 
- `Value of attribute`: The value of the attribute that we want to identify

In Xpath, we use the `//tagname` to select elements based on the tagname. Whereas in CSS Selector, we directly specify the `tagname` without any slashes.

In Xpath, we use `@attribute` to select the attribute of the element. Whereas in CSS Selector we directly specify as `attribute` without using `@`.

We know that CSS styles can be applied to different elements based on their class name, tag name, and ID.

```css
/* Applying styles based on class name */
.myClass {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 16px;
}

/* Applying styles based on tag name */
p {
  font-weight: bold;
}

/* Applying styles based on id */
#myId {
  background-color: yellow;
}
```

Similarly, we can select the web elements using CSS Selector based on their class name, tag name, and ID.

**1.1.1 Tag Name Selector**

Using tagname we can select elements based on their HTML tag names.

Let's understand this with the sample HTML code.

#### Example

Let's consider the following HTML code and select all the elements with `<div>` tag using the Tag Name Selector.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-11-sample-html.png" alt="tag name selector">

If we use `div` as CSS Selector, it selects all the elements with the tag name `div`.

Let's learn about the ID Selector and Class Selector

**1.1.2 ID Selector**

It is used to select the elements based on the ID attribute of the element. For specifying an ID, it uses the `#` symbol followed by the ID value.

#### Syntax

```java
tagname#id_value
```

**1.1.3 Class Selector**

It is used to select the elements based on their class attribute. It uses the `.`(dot)  symbol followed by the class name.

#### Syntax

```java
tagname.classValue
```

**1.1.4 Attribute Selector**

It is used to select the elements based on their specific attributes.

#### Syntax

```java
tagname[attribute= "Value of Attribute"]
```

It is considered a best practice to use ID selectors and class selectors when selecting elements based on their class names and ID attributes. For selecting elements based on other attributes, we can use the attribute selector.

Let us understand the above selectors with an example.

#### Example

Let's consider the sample HTML structure and select different elements using the Tag Name Selector, Class Selector, ID Selector, and Attribute Selector.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
 <head> </head>
 <body>
   <div id="parent1">
     <input type="text"
            name="fname"/>
     <div class="parent2">
       <p>Grandchild1</p>
     </div>
   </div>
 </body>
</html>
```

| Selector  |       Syntax        |    Example     |
| :-------: | :-----------------: | :------------: |
|    Tag    |       tagName       |      div       |
|    Id     |      #id_value      |  div#parent1   |
|   Class   |    .class_value     |  div.parent2   |
| Attribute | [attribute="value"] | [name="fname"] |

