### 1.2 Advanced Attribute Selectors

Similar to XPath functions, CSS selectors also provide ways to identify elements based on certain conditions or criteria.

These CSS selectors are used for locating the elements based on the partial values of attributes. These are used when the part of the attribute value changes dynamically.

The following are some of the CSS Attribute Selectors:

| CSS Attribute Selector |                             Description                             |
| :--------------------: | :-----------------------------------------------------------------: |
|        Contains        |  Locates elements whose attribute value contains a specific value   |
|      Starts with       | Locates elements whose attribute value starts with a specific value |
|       Ends with        |  Locates elements whose attribute value ends with a specific value  |

**Contains**

To locate the element based on partial attribute value, we should use the `*=` operator in CSS attribute selectors.

To locate the element based on partial attribute value in the XPath, we use the `contains()` function.

#### Syntax

```xml
tag[attribute *= ‘partial-value’]
```

Let's learn when to use the `*=` operator with the example.

#### Example

In real-world applications, we may encounter scenarios where the part of the attribute value changes dynamically.

<Img imageSrc="https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/images/qa-rm-11-sample-htmls.png" alt="dynamic html">

Let’s write a CSS Selector expression to identify `<p>` web element with an id attribute.

The CSS id selector expression would be, `p[id *= 'child-']`.

Let's update our existing code by using the `*=` operator to retrieve the job elements with `<li>` tag using the partial value of the class name as `job-it`.

```java
