### Summary

- CSS Selectors are used in Selenium to identify web elements based on their attributes, relationships with other elements, or specific element types.
- There are different types of CSS selectors in Selenium, including simple selectors, advanced attribute selectors, combinator selectors, and pseudo-class selectors.
- Simple selectors target elements based on their basic properties such as element type/tag, ID, class, or attribute.
   
    - Tag Name Selector: Select elements based on their HTML tag names.
    - ID Selector: Select elements based on their ID attribute using the `#` symbol followed by the ID value.
    - Class Selector: Select elements based on their class attribute using the `.` symbol followed by the class name.
    - Attribute Selector: Select elements based on specific attributes using the syntax `tagname[attribute='value']`.
- It is recommended to use ID selectors and class selectors when selecting elements based on their ID and class attributes, respectively.
- CSS Advanced Attribute Selectors provide a way to locate elements based on partial values of attributes. They are useful when the attribute values change dynamically.
         
    - Contains (`*=`): This selector locates elements whose attribute value contains a specific partial value. The syntax is `tag[attribute *= 'partial-value']`.
    - Starts with (`^=`): This selector locates elements whose attribute value starts with a specific partial value. The syntax is` tag[attribute ^= 'partial-value']`.

    - Ends with (`$=`): This selector locates elements whose attribute value ends with a specific partial value. The syntax is `tag[attribute $= 'partial-value']`.
</Section>

