## 1. CSS Selectors

We learned that CSS Selectors are one of the locator strategies offered by Selenium to identify web elements.

CSS selectors primarily target elements based on their attributes, relationships with other elements, or specific element types. CSS selectors are similar to XPath with some advantages and disadvantages that we will discuss later.

Selenium offers different types of CSS selectors that are categorized as

- Simple Selectors
- Advanced Attribute Selectors
- Combinator Selectors
- Pseudo-class Selectors

Let's learn how to test functionality of the jobs page and job details page of the Jobby application using the Pseudo-class selectors.

## 2. Pseudo-class Selectors

Similar to the positions in XPath, Pseudo-class selectors allow us to select elements based on their position in the document without the need for additional classes or IDs.

The most commonly used pseudo-class selectors are:

- first-child
- last-child
- nth-child
- first-of-type
- last-of-type
- nth-of-type

In most of the cases these psuedo-class selectors are used in context of a parent element. 

### 2.1 First-child

The `first-child` selector allows us to select the first-child element among a group of sibling elements, regardless of its type.

#### Syntax

```text
:first-child
```

let's understand the usage of `first-child` in the context of a parent element using the below example.

#### Example 

In the below HTML snippet, consider the `<div class="parent">` as the parent element. 

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>HTML Snippet</title>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="parent">
    <p class="child">Child 1</p>
    <p class="child">Child 2</p> 
    <div class="child">
      <p id="grandchild1">Grandchild 1</p>
    </div>
    <div class="child">
      <p id="grandchild2">Grandchild 2</p>
    </div>
    <p class="child">Child 3</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>

```

To select the first child of the given parent element, we can write the CSS selector expression as `div.parent > :first-child`.

Here, the expression `div.parent >` selects all the child elements of `<div class="parent">` and the `:first-child` selector selects the first child element inside the `<div class="parent">` i.e., `<p class="child">Child 1</p>`.

