### 2.3 Nth-child

The `nth-child` selector allows us to select elements based on their position among a group of sibling elements, regardless of its type.


#### Syntax

```text
:nth-child(expression)
```

Here,

- `element` represents the current node.
- `:nth-child(expression)` selects the nth child element based on the expression. 
- `expression` might be a _number_, or keyword like _even_, _odd_ or a formula like (an + b). The _number_ will always **starts from 1**.

#### Example 1

In the below HTML snippet, consider the `<div class="parent">` as the parent element. 

```html
<div class="parent">
  <p class="child">Child 1</p>
  <p class="child">Child 2</p> 
  <div class="child">
    <p id="grandchild1">Grandchild 1</p>
  </div>
  <div class="child">
    <p id="grandchild2">Grandchild 2</p>
  </div>
  <p class="child">Child 3</p>
</div>
```

To select the any nth(in our case, let's say second) child of the given parent element, we can write the CSS selector expression as `div.parent > :nth-child(2)`.

Here, the expression `div.parent >` selects all the child elements of `<div class="parent">` and the `:nth-child(2)` selector selects the second child element inside the `<div class="parent">` i.e., `<p class="child">Child 2</p>`

#### Example 2

In this example, let's try using the keyword `odd`

```html
<div class="parent">
  <p class="child">Child 1</p>
  <p class="child">Child 2</p> 
  <div class="child">
    <p id="grandchild1">Grandchild 1</p>
  </div>
  <div class="child">
    <p id="grandchild2">Grandchild 2</p>
  </div>
  <p class="child">Child 3</p>
</div>
```

To select the child elements that are at odd postions in the given parent element, we can write the CSS selector expression as `div.parent > :nth-child(odd)`.

Here, the expression `div.parent >` selects all the child elements of `<div class="parent">` and the `:nth-child(odd)` selector selects all the child elements inside the `<div class="parent">` that are at the odd postions(like 1, 3, 5, etc). In this case, the below elements are selected.

```
<p class="child">Child 1</p>
<div class="child">...</div>
<p class="child">Child 3</p>
```

Apart from numbers and keywords, we can create our own expressions using the formula `an + b`. Here, the number `a` represents the cycle size, the number `n` represents the counter (which starts at 0), and the number `b` represents the offset value.

| Expression | Description                                                                       |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 5n         | Selects every 5th child element                                                   |
| 3n + 1     | Selects every 3rd child element starting from the first child i.e., 1, 4, 7 ...   |
| 4n + 2     | Selects every 4th child element starting from the second child i.e., 2, 6, 10 ... |
| 5n - 1     | Selects every 5th child element subtracting 1 from its index i.e., 4, 9, 14 ...   |



