## 1. Alerts

An "Alert" is a small message box that appears on the screen to give the user some information or notification.

They display important information or prompt the user for confirmation or input. They are used to interact with the user and require the user's attention before proceeding further like specific information or error, ask for permission to perform certain tasks and it also provides warning messages as well.

In Selenium testing, major alerts can be categorized into two types:

- Web-based Alerts
- Window-based Alerts

In this unit, we will learn about the Web-based Alerts in detail.

</Section>

<Section>

## 2. Web-based Alerts

Web/Browser alerts are browser-dependent and appear in our web browser to provide important information or to ask for our response.

There are various types of web-based alerts that we can see in web applications:

1. Simple Alerts
2. Confirm Alerts
3. Prompt Alerts

Let's learn each of these types of alerts,

### 2.1 Simple Alerts

Simple alerts are just informational alerts and usually consist of an **OK** button. They are typically used to provide some information or warning to the user.

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/dbc9s4sim/image/upload/v1689333368/QA/handling-alerts/rm-alert_nslwhv.png" alt="alert" widthPercent="50%">

#### Example

let's take an example of a registration page. If the user tries to submit the form without filling out any of the fields, the website might display an alert saying "Please fill all the required fields."


<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/dbc9s4sim/image/upload/v1689339799/QA/handling-alerts/rm-registration-alert_yknbzq.png" alt="alert registration" widthPercent="30%">

### 2.2 Confirmation Alert

Confirmation alerts get a confirmation from the user in the form of accepting or dismissing the message box like **OK** and **Cancel**.

If clicked on **OK** - Accepting
If clicked on **Cancel** - Dismissing

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/dbc9s4sim/image/upload/v1689339944/QA/handling-alerts/rm-confirmation-alert_s6yuzm.png" alt="alert" widthPercent="50%">

#### Example

For example, if the user attempts to log out from their account, a confirmation alert might appear saying, "Are you sure you want to log out?"

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/dbc9s4sim/image/upload/v1689341993/QA/handling-alerts/rm-confirmation-alert-ex_glljrq.png" alt="alert" widthPercent="30%">

### 2.3 Prompt Alert

Prompt Alert asks some input from the user and Selenium webdriver can enter the text using sendkeys(" input…. ").

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/dbc9s4sim/image/upload/v1689340216/QA/handling-alerts/rm-prompt-alert_iqk2cb.png" alt="alert" widthPercent="50%">

For example, A website might have certain data that is password-protected. When we try to access this data, a prompt alert might appear asking us to "Enter the password".

Now, let's learn more about alerts in Selenium webdriver.

