<Section>

## 3. Handling Alerts in Selenium

When we use Selenium WebDriver to execute an automation script, the WebDriver focuses only on the main browser window. It runs all the commands on the main browser window only

But, whenever an alert appears, it opens up a new window. So, to handle the Alerts using Selenium WebDriver, the focus needs to be shifted to the child windows opened by the Alerts.

The selenium web driver provides the `switchTo()` method to switch the control between windows, frames, alerts, etc.

It returns an instance of the `TargetLocator` interface, which provides methods for switching the /control/focus between windows, frames alerts, etc.

| Method   | Decription                                   |
| :------- | :------------------------------------------- |
| alert()  | Switches to the currently active alert       |
| frame​() | Selects a frame based on index, name or id   |
| window() | Selects a window based on the name or handle |

In this unit, we will learn about the `alert()` method.

> We'll learn more about `frame()` and `window()` methods later in the course.


### 3.1 alert()

The `TargetLocator` interface consists of a method named `alert()` to switch the control to the active alert.

The `alert()` method returns an instance of the `Alert` type, which is an interface.

We can use the following command to switch our control to the active alert.

#### Syntax

```java
Alert alertObj = driver.switchTo().alert();
```

Here, 

- `Alert` is the interface.
- `driver` is any selenium web-driver object. For example _ChromeDriver_.
- `switchTo()` is to method to switch the control between windows, frames, alerts, etc. It returns the `TargetLocator` interface.
- `alert()` is the method to switch the control to the active alert. It returns an instance of the `Alert` type. 

The `Alert` interface from the `org.openqa.selenium` package in Selenium WebDriver offers methods that facilitate interactions with web page alert boxes (like accepting, dismissing, and retrieving text from them).

| Method    | Description                                     |
| :-------- | :---------------------------------------------- |
| getText() | Retrieves the message displayed in the alert box |
| accept()  | Clicks the 'OK' button inside the alert box      |
| dismiss() | Clicks the 'Cancel' or 'Close' button inside the alert box |

The above are the most commonly used methods to interact with the alert box.

Let's handle alerts in our Jobby Application.

