## Introduction

Imagine you're playing a game of catching a ball with your friend. You throw the ball and your friend has to catch it. But what if you throw the ball too quickly before your friend is ready to catch it? The ball will fall to the ground and your friend will miss it. This is similar to what happens in Selenium testing. Selenium (you in this example) sometimes runs too quickly for the website it's testing (your friend). So, Selenium might try to test a part of the website before that part has fully loaded, causing the test to fail.

To solve this problem, we use something called "Synchronization". 

In this unit, we'll learn what is "Synchronization" and the types of synchronization.

<MultiLineNote>

Click [here](https://new-assets.ccbp.in/frontend/content/qa-testing/WaitsPracticeTest.zip) to download the initial code for this project.
</MultiLineNote>

</Section>

<Section>

## 1. Synchronization

Synchronization is a process that helps in making two or more components work together at the same pace. In Selenium, synchronization is used to help the `WebDriver` and the web application work in sync.

When we run a Selenium script, sometimes the `WebDriver` runs faster than the web application. This can cause the `WebDriver` to perform an action on a web element that is not loaded yet, leading to errors in the test. To avoid these errors, we use synchronization to make the `WebDriver` wait until the web application is ready.

Synchronization is like telling you to wait until your friend is ready before you throw the ball. In Selenium, synchronization makes sure that Selenium waits until the website is ready before it runs the tests.

In Selenium automation testing, there are two things that should work in parallel or in sync:

- Application Under Test (AUT)
- Testing tool (Selenium)

**Types of Synchronization**

There are two types of synchronization in Selenium:

1. Conditional Synchronization
2. Unconditional Synchronization

</Section>

<Section>

## 2. Conditional Synchronization

In conditional synchronization, we specify a condition along with the timeout value. The `WebDriver` waits to check for the condition and then decides to continue the test flow or wait until the timeout exceeds. These conditions could be the visibility of an element, an element to be clickable, a certain text to be present in an element, and so on.

In selenium, conditional synchronization is divided into three types: 

1. Implicit Wait
2. Explicit Wait
3. Fluent Wait

### 2.1 Implicit Wait

Implicit Wait is a way to instruct the `WebDriver` to wait for a certain amount of time before it throws an exception ("No Such Element Exception"). The default setting is **0**. Once it is set, the implicit wait is set for the life of the `WebDriver` object instance until it's changed again.

In other words, if the `WebDriver` cannot find an element immediately because of its availability, it will keep polling (checking) the DOM to see if the element appears in the specified amount of time. This can be useful when certain elements on the webpage are not immediately available and are loaded at a later point in time.

#### Syntax

```java
driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));
```

Here, `Duration` is a class provided by Java that has multiple methods to represent a span of time in different units (ofSeconds(), ofMillis(), etc.)

Essentially, it sets a timeout that will be applied to all subsequent WebDriver.findElement()/WebDriver.findElements() calls and operations performed on the returned WebElement(s)

Let's understand it better by applying it in our Jobby Application.

To avoid repeating the above steps every time, we can utilize the initial code provided in the [Introduction](#introduction) section.

The initial code provided will automate the following steps:

- Logging into the application
- Navigating to the home page
- Navigating to the jobs page and verifying the display of jobs

Let's update our initial code with the below snippet immediately after declaring the driver object:

```java
driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));
```

Here, the wait will be applied for all `WebDriver.findElement()/WebDriver.findElements()` calls and operations performed on the returned WebElement(s).

We have the below statements in our initial code where we have used the `findElement()` method

