<Section>

## 3. Differences Among Waits

### 3.1 Difference Between Implicit and Explicit Wait

|                                         Implicit Wait                                          |                                     Explicit Wait                                     |
| :--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------: | :-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------: |
|                          Defined only a single time for all test flow                          |                              Can be defined per element                               |
|                          Applies to all elements in the being tested                           |         Can be applied based on the specific elements or specific conditions          |
| Most effective when elements would be located within the time frame specified in implicit wait | Most effective when used when the elements in the page are taking a long time to load |

### 3.2 Differences Among Implicit, Explicit Wait, and Fluent Wait

| Implicit Wait                                                           | Explicit Wait                                                      | Fluent Wait                                                             |
| :---------------------------------------------------------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Global for the entire WebDriver instance's lifetime                    | Specific to the element or condition where it is applied.          | Specific to the element or condition where it is applied.               |
| Defined once and applies to all elements in the test scenario           | Defined individually for each element or condition                 | Defined individually for each element or condition                      |
| The `implicitlyWait()` method of the `WebDriver.Timeouts` interface is used | The `WebDriverWait` class provides `until()` and `wait()` methods  | The `FluentWait` class provides `until()` and `pollingEvery()` methods  |
| Throws `NoSuchElementException` if element is not found                 | Throws `TimeoutException` if the condition is not met              | Throws `TimeoutException` if the condition is not met                   |
| Less flexible as it applies to all the elements                         | More flexible as it allows defining custom conditions and timeouts | More flexible as it allows defining custom conditions and timeouts      |
| Suitable for stable websites with consistent loading times              | Suitable for handling dynamic elements                             | Suitable for handling complex scenarios and unpredictable loading times |

</Section>
<Section>

### Summary

- Synchronization is a process that helps in making two or more components work together at the same pace. 
- Conditional Synchronization
  - _Implicit Wait:_ Implicit Wait is a way to instruct the WebDriver to wait for a certain amount of time before it throws an exception.
    - Once it is set, the implicit wait is set for the life of the WebDriver object instance until it's changed again.
  - _Explicit Wait:_ It is a type of synchronization mechanism where the WebDriver waits until certain conditions occur or the time duration is elapsed before proceeding with the next steps in the test script or throws a `TimeoutException`.
    - It provides better control compared to implicit wait as it allows you to specify wait conditions for specific elements or situations.
  - _Fluent Wait:_ It is similar to explicit wait in Selenium with one additional argument of frequency.

</Section>

