## 3. Writing the First Test Case in TestNG

In this unit, we will test the successful login case of the NxtTrendz Application using the TestNG Framework

For successful login, we may follow the below steps:

1. Open Chrome browser
2. Enter the URL of the application in browser
3. Enter username and password
4. Click on the login button
5. User should be able to redirect to the Home page

Let's automate all the above steps. Firstly, let's create a class and name it as `LoginPageTest`.

Previously we have written test case without any annotations and used `main()` method to run the tests, now in TestNG we use annotations and write the test cases in seperate methods annotated with `@Test`.

<MultiLineNote>

**Annotations** are a form of metadata that provide additional information about classes, methods, fields, etc. They do not directly affect the program's logic but can be used by various tools, frameworks, or the runtime environment to perform specific actions or configurations.

Annotations start with the `@` symbol and are placed immediately before the program element (like methods, attributes) they are intended to annotate. Annotations can accept parameters and arguments, depending on their purpose.
</MultiLineNote>

TestNG provides a test framework that manages the execution of these test methods. We don't need to use the main method for executing test cases.

To use the `@Test` annotation we need to import it from the package `org.testng.annotations.Test` as below

#### Sample Code

```text
...

public class LoginPageTest {
    @Test
    public void loginWithValidCredentials() {

    }  
}
```

In TestNG, the `@Test` annotation is used to mark a method as a test method. When we annotate a method with `@Test`, TestNG treats it as a test case and executes it during test execution.

<MultiLineWarning>

It is essential to annotate our test methods in TestNG to run the tests. TestNG will ignore the method which does not contain an annotation.
</MultiLineWarning>

Now lets write our selenium code with TestNG and automate process upto submitting the login form with credentials.

#### Code

```java
import org.testng.annotations.Test;

import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;

public class LoginPageTest {
    @Test
    public void loginWithValidCredentials() {
        // Set the system property for Chrome driver with its path
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

        // Create a new instance of ChromeDriver
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to the login page
        driver.get("https://rahulnxttrendz.ccbp.tech/login");

        // Locate the username input field element by its id
        WebElement usernameEl = driver.findElement(By.id("username"));
        usernameEl.sendKeys("rahul");

        // Locate the password input field element by its id
        WebElement passwordEl = driver.findElement(By.id("password"));
        passwordEl.sendKeys("rahul@2021");

        // Find the login button element by its class name
        WebElement loginButtonEl = driver.findElement(By.className("login-button"));

        // Submit the login form
        loginButtonEl.submit();

    }
}
```

