<Section>

## 2. Adding Priority

In TestNG, we can specify the order in which our test cases should be executed using the `priority` attribute in the `@Test` annotation. The `priority` attribute takes an integer value, and test cases with lower priority values are executed before test cases with higher priority values.

By default, test methods without a `priority` value are assigned the default value of `0`

Let's add priority to our test cases. We will give the `loginWithValidCredentials` test case a priority of 1, and the `loginWithInvalidCredentials` test case a priority of 2. This means the `loginWithValidCredentials` test case will be executed first, followed by the `loginWithInvalidCredentials` test case.

```java
@Test(priority = 1)
public void loginWithValidCredentials() {
    // Test case code...
}

@Test(priority = 2)
public void loginWithInvalidCredentials() {
    // Test case code...
}
```

By adding priority to our test cases, we can control the order in which they are executed, which can be useful in certain testing scenarios.

