## 1. Page Object Model

The page object model is a design pattern that reduces code duplication and improves test case maintenance. 

The idea behind POM is to create a separate Java Class for each web page in the application. These classes are known as **page objects**(pages).

Each Page Object is responsible for storing all the web elements information of that page and the actions that can be performed on these elements(Elements Information: Locators to identify web elements like ID, name, class, etc.)

For Example, let's see what Page Object or Page Class can contain for the login page

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/duklcooq4/image/upload/v1691217892/POM/Screenshot_from_2023-08-05_12-13-56_lqjptr.png" alt="login-page-object">


The **Web elements** we would commonly use during our test are: 

 - Username input 
 - Password input 
 - Login Button 

And the **actions** that can be **performed** on these elements can be

 - enterUsername()
 - enterPassword()
 - clickOnLoginButton()

Once we define the page objects for each page based on the functionality, we can instantiate these page classes in our test cases as required

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/duklcooq4/image/upload/v1691218515/POM/Screenshot_from_2023-08-05_12-24-50_qtdu0h.png" alt="login-page-object">


This approach helps maintain our test code by separating the page-specific code (like locators and actions on elements) from the test code
It makes our tests cleaner and easier to understand

### 1.1 Advantages

*Reusable:* Placing all the web elements of each page in a separate class makes it reusable by making it accessible by any test script

*Maintainable:* Since the test classes are separate from the classes containing the web elements and the operation on them, updating the code becomes easy 

*Readable:* Users can easily read through the project and test scripts due to a fine line between the test classes and the different web pages

Now, we have a basic understanding of POM. Let's see how can we implement POM.
