## 1. Page Factory in Selenium

Page Factory is an enhancement of the **Simple POM**, provided by Selenium itself. It simplifies the creation and management of Page Objects, making the automation code more `efficient`, `readable`, and `maintainable`.

In this approach, we will use a class called `PageFactory` provided by the Selenium to implement POM.

Instead of manually creating Page Objects and initializing web elements `(like buttons, text fields, etc.)` on each page, we use the PageFactory class provided by Selenium. This class `automatically initializes` the web elements using `annotations`, which are special instructions marked within the `Page Object class`.

The `Page Factory` class provides annotations to locate elements directly in the page class, without having to write a `driver.findElement()` method:

- **@FindBy**
- **@FindBys**

These annotations enable us to locate web elements directly within the page class, eliminating the need for writing the **driver.findElement()** method. 

These annotations simplify the process of defining and initializing web elements in the Page Object Model, making the automation code more concise and efficient.

### @FindBy
- `@FindBy` is an annotation used in Page Factory to locate and declare web elements using different locators. This replaces the way we located elements in the normal `POM` approach.
- Unlike the regular approach of initializing web page elements using `FindElement` or `FindElements`, the Page Factory uses the **@FindBy** annotation

#### Syntax

```java
@FindBy(locatorStrategy="value") WebElement element;
```
#### Code
```java
@FindBy(id="username") WebElement userNameEle;

@FindBy(id="password") WebElement passwordEle;

@FindBy(className="login-button") WebElement submitButtonEle;

```

When we need to interact with the elements in our tests, we can directly call the interaction methods like `sendKeys()` or `click()`.

We don't need to call the **driver.findElement()** every time

```java
@FindBy(id="username") WebElement userNameEle;

userNameEle.sendKeys(uname);
```

### Simple Approach & Page Factory

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/ddxyfxwzy/image/upload/v1691472317/Screenshot_from_2023-08-08_10-53-45_ogxncw.png" alt="simple pom & pagefactory">

- In the simple `Page Object Model (POM)` approach, locating web elements involves creating a locator object, like `usernameLocator`, using the By class and specifying the locating strategy (e.g., ID). This locator is then used within methods like `enterUserName()` to find the element using the `driver.findElement()` method. This approach can result in more code, as locators need to be defined separately, and each method requires calling `driver.findElement()`.

- On the other hand, with the Page Factory's `@FindBy` annotation, both the declaration and location of web elements, as well as their interaction, are simplified. The `@FindBy` annotation directly associates a web element with a `variable, like usernameEle`. The annotation specifies the locator strategy and value (e.g., ID) to find the element. This approach eliminates the need for separate locator objects and reduces the code required for both finding and interacting with elements.

