<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/ddxyfxwzy/image/upload/v1691485164/Screenshot_from_2023-08-08_14-29-13_unlzkd.png" alt="output">

### Lazy Loading:
Both the Simple approach and the PageFactory model involve a form of **lazy initialization**, where the actual finding of elements is delayed until they're needed. However, the methods of achieving this are distinct. The PageFactory model utilizes a concept called proxies to enhance code clarity and readability.

In the PageFactory model, when you declare a WebElement field using `@FindBy`, it appears and behaves like a regular element in your code. However, under the surface, these fields are implemented as proxies. This means that they don't directly locate the element during initialization. Instead, they wait until you invoke a method or perform an action on the field.

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.support.FindBy;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.How;

public class LoginPageUsingPageFactory {
  ...
  @FindBy(how = How.ID, using="passwordInput")
  WebElement passwordEle;

  @FindBy(how = How.CLASS_NAME, using="login-button")
  WebElement submitButton;

  @FindBy(how = How.CLASS_NAME, using="error-message")
  WebElement errorMessageEle;
}
```
As `FindBy` is not actually searches the DOM for the elements immediately, we are not getting any error when declaring error message element before it appears in the DOM

### @CacheLookup Annotation

When we are referring to the same web element multiple times on a web page, we can use the `@CacheLookUp` annotation.

The `@CacheLookup` annotation is used in conjunction with `@FindBy or @FindBys` annotation in PageFactory to cache the element once it's located.When using this, it will not check this web elementeach and every time on the web page this will result in high performance


#### Syntax

```java
@FindBy(id="userNameInput")
@Cachelookup
WebElement userNameEle; 
```
When using the `@CacheLookup` annotation, web elements needed for common operations like login are found and stored in memory the first time they're accessed. 

Subsequent uses of these elements in other test cases avoid re-searching the DOM, resulting in faster test execution. However, use it wisely for elements that don't change, as cached references can become stale.

#### Example

```java
...
public class LoginPageUsingPageFactory { 
  ...
  @FindBy(id="username")
  @Cachelookup
  WebElement userNameEle;
  ...
}
```

</Section>

<Section>

## Advantages of Page Factory

- **Readability:** Page Factory enhances code clarity by centralizing element declarations at the top of classes, making it easy to identify and understand the elements present on a page.

- **Maintenance:** With Page Factory, if an element's location changes, you only need to update the corresponding annotation, eliminating the need to modify multiple places in your code.

- **Reduced Duplication:** Page Factory minimizes repetitive driver.findElement() calls by automatically initializing elements using annotations, resulting in concise and efficient code.

- **Ease of Use:** Initializing elements with Page Factory is straightforward, leading to a more intuitive coding experience, especially for newcomers to automation.

</Section>

<Section>

## Summary

- **Page Factory:** Page Factory in Page Object Model concept for Selenium WebDriver is used to initialize the elements of the Page Object or instantiate the Page Objects itself. It makes our code cleaner and more maintainable.
- **@FindBy**: This annotation is used in Page Factory to locate and declare web elements using different attributes like id, name, className, etc.
- **Implementing POM using Page Factory:** The process involves defining a separate class for each page, using @FindBy to declare web elements, and using the PageFactory class to initialize these elements. Then, we define methods to perform actions on the web elements. Finally, we use the page object in our test class to perform actions and run our tests.
</Section>

