## 2. Switching Between Windows

When we open multiple windows/tabs in our application, to perform actions on the new child window, we need to move our focus to the new child window created. Selenium provides methods to move our focus to the new child window.

#### Syntax

```java
driver.switchTo().window(ChildWindow);
```

Here, the `window()` method accepts the child window's unique identifier as an argument. 

When we open a new window and get the window handles using the `getWindowHandles()` method, we will get two window handles. But we have to identify which window handle belongs to which window. To do so, we can store the parent window handle in a variable and while iterating over the window handles, we can compare the parent window handle with the window handles we have got and check for _not_ condition.

To understand it better, let's get back to our web application and try switching between the windows.

#### Code

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

import java.util.Set;

public class MultipleWindowsTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get("https://qahndlwindows.ccbp.tech/");

        // Get the parent window handle
        String parentWindowHandle = driver.getWindowHandle();

        // Click on the "Open New Window" button
        WebElement newWindowBtn = driver.findElement(By.id("newWindow"));
        newWindowBtn.click();

        // get all window handles
        Set<String> windowHandlesSet = driver.getWindowHandles();

        for(String windowHandle : windowHandlesSet) {
            if(!(windowHandle.equals(parentWindowHandle))) {
                // switching to new window
                driver.switchTo().window(windowHandle);

                String title = driver.getTitle();
                System.out.println(title);
            }
        }

        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```

Google
```


In the above code, we have stored the parent window handle and wrote a condition to check if the window handle is not equal to the parent window handle. In this way, we identified and switched to the child window and printed the title onto the console.

Similarly, we can switch to a new tab. Let's try it out with our code.

