## 3. Closing Windows

The Selenium WebDriver provides two methods to close browser windows. They are, 

- `close()`
- `quit()`

### 3.1 Close()

The `close()` method in Selenium WebDriver is used to close the current browser window or tab that has focus. If we have multiple browser windows or tabs open, and we execute this command, only the active or focused tab or window will be closed. If it's the only open window or tab, the browser will be closed entirely.

If the closed window or tab is the last one, the browser will be closed, but the WebDriver session will not be terminated. **This means we can still use the same WebDriver instance to open new browser windows or tabs**.

If we try to close a browser session that's already closed, WebDriver might throw a `NoSuchWindowException`.

#### Syntax

```java
driver.close()
```

Now, let's try it out with our web application

#### Code

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

import java.util.Set;

public class MultipleWindowsTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "PATH_TO_CHROMEDRIVER");

        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get("https://qahndlwindows.ccbp.tech/");

        // Get the parent window handle
        String parentWindowHandle = driver.getWindowHandle();

        // Click on the "Open New Tab" button
        WebElement newWindowBtn = driver.findElement(By.id("newWindow"));
        newWindowBtn.click();

        // get all window handles
        Set<String> windowHandlesSet = driver.getWindowHandles();

        for(String windowHandle : windowHandlesSet) {
            if(!(windowHandle.equals(parentWindowHandle))) {
                // switching to new window
                driver.switchTo().window(windowHandle);

                String title = driver.getTitle();
                System.out.println(title);

                driver.close();
            }
        }

        // get all window handles
        windowHandlesSet = driver.getWindowHandles();

        System.out.println(windowHandlesSet);

        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Google
[749099D6824AC761009C1C3569104C1B]
```

In the above code, we have navigated to our web application and opened a new window tab. Then we switched our focus to the new window and printed the title onto the console. Later, we closed the child window session using the `close()` method.

As one window has been closed, we can notice that only one window handle has been printed in the console.

Similarly, when we open and new tab and use `close()` on the new tab, it closes the tab. _Try this scenario on your local system_.

### 3.2 Quit()

The `quit()` method in Selenium WebDriver is used to terminate the web browser session. 

When invoked, it closes all windows and tabs associated with that WebDriver instance and safely ends the session. This ensures that all associated browser processes are terminated, freeing up system resources. Using `quit()` is essential for cleanup after the automation tasks are completed.

#### Syntax

```java
driver.quit()
```

Now, let's try it out with our web application

