## Introduction

Selenium WebDriver has become a key tool in web automation testing, allowing us to simulate user interactions with precision and ease. 

In this session, we will explore three essential aspects of Selenium WebDriver: `Browser Navigation`, `WebElement Commands`, and `Browser Commands`. Together, these concepts empower testers to navigate web pages, interact with various elements, and control browsers, forming the foundation of effective automation testing.



## 1. Browser Navigation Commands in Selenium WebDriver

Imagine driving a car. We have a steering wheel to control the direction, pedals to move or stop, and gears to navigate backward or forward. 

Similarly, when automating a web browser using Selenium WebDriver, we need specific commands to navigate and control the browser's direction.

In web automation, it's often necessary to navigate to different pages, go back to a previously visited page, move forward, or even refresh the current page. 

These actions replicate the real-world user behavior of browsing the internet. To simulate this in automation scripts, Selenium provides a set of navigation commands. 

These commands allow us to guide Web automation script just like a driver guiding a car.

Now, let's delve deeper into the specific browser navigation commands available in Selenium WebDriver and see how they operate.


### 1.1 Navigating to a URL

Opening a URL is like telling the robot to type a web address in the browser and press Enter.

Web browsers typically have an address bar where we can type in a URL and navigate to that specific website. The `driver.navigate().to()` method in Selenium WebDriver simulates this action.

`driver.navigate().to("URL")` method directs the browser to navigate to the specified URL, just as if we typed it into the browser's address bar and hit Enter. It's functionally similar to the `driver.get("URL")` method.

#### Code:
```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class SeleniumExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Set the path to the chromedriver executable
        // Note: Replace '/path/to/chromedriver' with the actual path on Web system
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "/path/to/chromedriver");

        // Create a new instance of ChromeDriver
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to another URL using the navigate().to() method
        driver.navigate().to("https://rahulnxttrendz.ccbp.tech/login");

        // Close the browser after tasks are completed
        driver.quit();
    }
}
```



### 1.2 Navigating Back and Forward


In a web browser, we often click the "back" button to return to the previous page, or the "forward" button to advance to the next page in web browsing history. Selenium WebDriver provides methods to simulate these actions.

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/ddxyfxwzy/image/upload/v1691664611/Screenshot_from_2023-08-10_16-20-04_et266n.png" alt="navigating back and forward">


-  `driver.navigate().back()`: method replicates the action of clicking the "back" button in the browser. It takes us to the previously visited page in web browsing history.

- `driver.navigate().forward()`: method replicates the action of clicking the "forward" button in the browser. If we've navigated back, we can use this method to move forward in web browsing history again.

#### Code

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;

public class SeleniumExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Set the path to the chromedriver executable
        // Note: Replace '/path/to/chromedriver' with the actual path on Web system
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "/path/to/chromedriver");

        // Create a new instance of ChromeDriver
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to another URL using the navigate().to() method
        driver.navigate().to("https://rahulnxttrendz.ccbp.tech/login");


        String cuttentUrl = driver.getCurrentUrl();
        System.out.println(cuttentUrl);

        // Navigate to another URL using the navigate().to() method
        driver.navigate().to("https://facebook.com");

        cuttentUrl = driver.getCurrentUrl();
        System.out.println(cuttentUrl);

        // Navigate back (this will take We back to "https://rahulnxttrendz.ccbp.tech/login")
        driver.navigate().back();

        cuttentUrl = driver.getCurrentUrl();
        System.out.println(cuttentUrl);

        // Navigate forward (this will take We to "https://facebook.com" again)
        driver.navigate().forward();

        cuttentUrl = driver.getCurrentUrl();
        System.out.println(cuttentUrl);

        // Close the browser after tasks are completed
        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

