## 1. iframe in Web Application

Imagine you have a toy box, and inside that toy box, you have another smaller toy box. This smaller toy box can have its own set of toys, different from the main box. In the world of web pages, an iframe is like that smaller toy box inside the big one. It's a way to insert another document within the main webpage.

For example, when you see a video from YouTube embedded in another website, that video is likely inside an iFrame!

An *iframe* short for inline frame is an HTML element on the web page that allows us to embed different kinds of media like images, documents, videos inside the main web page. An iframe HTML element is defined using `<iframe>` tag in HTML

The media can be internal images or documents related to the website or external sources from other websites

This can be useful when you want to display external content, such as advertisements, videos, maps, or other interactive elements, seamlessly within our own website

*For Example:* 

**Video Embedding:** Platforms like YouTube provide an embed code that allows us to display videos from their sites on your webpage. By using an iFrame, we can seamlessly integrate videos from the another websites so that users can watch them without navigating away from your website.

**Adversitement Banners:** Advertisements from ad networks can be easily integrated using iFrames. Website owners can earn revenue by embedding these ad banners within their pages, which display targeted ads to users.

Let's see how can we handle/automate iframes in selenium
</Section>

<Section>

## 2. Handling iframes Using Selenium

As we already learnt in previous sessions that when we use Selenium WebDriver to execute an automation script, the WebDriver focuses only on the main browser window. It runs all the commands on the main browser window only. 

Selenium WebDriver can't access and locate the web elements inside the iframes, alerts, etc directly

Let's understand the same with the help of an example on the web page [NxtApps](https://qanxtapps.ccbp.tech/). The application displays the list of applications we have built. By default, todo application will be displayed in the iframe. And on click of the each application the specific application will be loaded an iframe.

<Img imageSrc="https://res.cloudinary.com/duklcooq4/image/upload/v1691650145/iframes/iframe-1_nd6i6p.gif" alt="app-page">

If we try to access the web element with text `Todos` we will get an error as the selenium cannot access the elements inside the iframe directly.

So, the selenium webdriver first needs to switch the context to the *iframe* to access all the web elements inside the iframe. Let's see how can we access the elements inside the iframe.

To access the elements present in the iframe using Selenium WebDriver, the focus need to be shifted to the iframe.

The selenium webdriver provides the `switchTo()` to method to switch the control between windows, frames and alerts, etc.

It returns an instance of the `TargetLocator` interface, which provides methods for switching the focus of the webdriver between windows, frames and alerts, etc.

| Method   | Decription                                   |
| :------- | :------------------------------------------- |
| alert()  | Switches to the currently active alert       |
| frame​() | Selects a frame based on index, name, id or web element   |
| window() | Selects a window based on the name or handle |

In this unit, we will learn about `frame()` method.

### 2.1 frame()

The `TargetLocator` interface consists of a method named `frame()` to switch the control to the iframe.

Selenium WebDriver provides three ways to switch the focus to a specified iframe:

1. Using Name or Id of the iframe
2. Using the Web Element object of the iframe
3. Using Index of the iframe

Let's see how can we switch context to iframe using iframe name or id (We can get the id or name of the iframe by checking the DOM of the page)

We can use the following command to switch our control to the iframe.

#### Syntax

```java
driver.switchTo().frame(IFRAME_NAME or IFRAME_ID)
```

Here, 

- `driver` is any selenium web-driver object. For example _ChromeDriver_.
- `switchTo()` is a method to switch the control between windows, frames and alerts, etc. It returns `TargetLocator` interface.
- `frame()` is the method to switch the control to the iframe

Let’s understand the handling of iframes in automation testing by using the NxtApps application (`https://qanxtapps.ccbp.tech/`)

