# XPath Operators

<Section>

## Concepts
<IndexList>
<IndexItem href="1-xpath-operators">XPath Operators</IndexItem>
<IndexItem href="2-types-of-xpath-operators">Types of XPath Operators</IndexItem>
</IndexList>

</Section>

<Section>


## Introduction

Sometimes we find ourselves in situations where we need to write complex queries to locate elements within XML or HTML documents. In such cases, XPath boolean and comparison operators come to the rescue by providing efficient and powerful tools to navigate these structures.

## 1. XPath Operators

XPath operators allow us to refine our search and locate elements or data within an HTML document with precision.

## 2. Types of XPath Operators

- Boolean Operators
- Comparison Operators

### 2.1 Boolean Operators

In XPath, boolean operators are utilized to combine or modify boolean values, typically resulting from certain conditions or tests.

- `and` operator is used when you want to check if `both of the conditions are true`.

- `or` operator is used when you want to check if `at least one of the conditions is true`.

- `not()` function inverts the result of a boolean condition. If a condition inside the not() function evaluates to true, not() will return false and vice versa.

let's focus on the boolean operators AND, OR, and NOT in XPath using [this](https://qaxpathoperator.ccbp.tech/) application.

#### Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>XPath Operators Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="main">
        <p class="intro" data-number="5">This is an intro paragraph.</p>
        <p data-number="10">This is a paragraph with a number 10.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="footer">
        <span class="note" hidden>Note: This is a hidden note.</span>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```


```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;

public class BooleanOperatorsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Set the path for chromedriver if needed
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "path_to_chromedriver");
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to a website
        driver.get("https://qaxpathoperator.ccbp.tech/");

        // Boolean AND Operator: fetch a paragraph with class 'intro' and data-number attribute
        String introParagraphText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@class='intro' and @data-number='5']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Boolean AND Operator: " + introParagraphText);

        // Boolean OR Operator: fetch a paragraph with class 'intro' or data-number as 10
        introParagraphText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@class='intro' or @data-number='10']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Boolean OR Operator: " + introParagraphText);

        // Boolean NOT Operator: fetch any element which does not have the 'hidden' attribute
        introParagraphText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[not(@hidden)]")).getText();
        System.out.println("Boolean NOT Operator: " + introParagraphText);

        // Close the browser after task completion
        driver.quit();
    }
}
```
#### Output

```
Boolean AND Operator: This is an intro paragraph
Boolean OR Operator: This is an intro paragraph
Boolean NOT Operator: This is an intro paragraph
```

### 2.2 Comparison Operators

Comparison operators in XPath allow us to compare one operand to another. These operands could be numbers, strings, or other node sequences.

1. `= (Equals To)` : Checks if the attribute value is equal to the specified value.
2. `!=(Not Equals To)` : Checks if the attribute value is not equal to the specified value.
3. `< (Less Than)` : Checks if the attribute value is less than the specified value.
4. `> (Greater Than)` : Checks if the attribute value is greater than the specified value.
5. `<= (Less Than or Equal To)` : Checks if the attribute value is less than or equal to the specified value.
6. `>= (Greater Than or Equal To)` : Checks if the attribute value is greater than or equal to the specified value.

Let's use the comparison operators with [this](https://qaxpathoperator.ccbp.tech/) application. 

#### Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>XPath Operators Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="main">
        <p class="intro" data-number="5">This is an intro paragraph with number 5.</p>
        <p data-number="10">This is a paragraph with a number 10.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="footer">
        <span class="note" hidden>Note: This is a hidden note.</span>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.By;

public class ComparisonOperatorsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Set the path for chromedriver if needed
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "path_to_chromedriver");

        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to a website
        driver.get("https://qaxpathoperator.ccbp.tech/");

        // Equals To Operator
        String equalsToText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number='5']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Equals To Operator: " + equalsToText);

        // Not Equals To Operator
        String notEqualsToText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number!='5']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Not Equals To Operator: " + notEqualsToText);

        // Less Than Operator
        String lessThanText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number<'10']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Less Than Operator: " + lessThanText);

        // Greater Than Operator
        String greaterThanText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number>'5']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Greater Than Operator: " + greaterThanText);

        // Less Than or Equals To Operator
        String lessThanOrEqualsToText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number<='5']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Less Than or Equals To Operator: " + lessThanOrEqualsToText);

        // Greater Than or Equals To Operator
        String greaterThanOrEqualsToText = driver.findElement(By.xpath("//p[@data-number>='10']")).getText();
        System.out.println("Greater Than or Equals To Operator: " + greaterThanOrEqualsToText);

        // Close the browser after task completion
        driver.close();
    }
}
```

#### Output

```
Equals To Operator: This is an intro paragraph.
Not Equals To Operator: This is a paragraph with a number 10.
Less Than Operator: This is an intro paragraph.
Greater Than Operator: This is a paragraph with a number 10.
Less Than or Equals To Operator: This is an intro paragraph.
Greater Than or Equals To Operator: This is a paragraph with a number 10.

```

##Summary

- These operators in XPath help in formulating precise queries to select nodes from XML or HTML documents. They are especially crucial in tasks such as web scraping and test automation to filter and pinpoint specific elements based on conditions or data attributes.

- Boolean operators are used to combine or negate conditions for more specific choices.

- Comparison operators are used to compare two values or expressions to evaluate to a true or false result.

</Section>

