## 2. Scroll Action

Scrolling is one of the most important features of any application. You will not find any website which does not have scrolling capability.

**Lazy Loading**: Some websites use lazy loading to load additional content only when the user scrolls to a certain point on the page. In these cases, the elements are not yet present in the DOM until the page is scrolled, triggering the API call to load the content.

**JavaScript-based Interactions**: Some websites may have JavaScript-based interactions that only get triggered when an element is scrolled into view. For example, animations or other visual effects might be applied to elements as they come into view.

**User Experience Testing**: When testing the user experience, it's important to simulate the actual user interactions, which may include scrolling to access certain parts of the page.

In Selenium, scrolling can be handled by using `JavaScriptExecutor`. JavaScriptExecutor is an `interface` that is used to execute JavaScript through selenium webdriver. 

JavaScriptExecutor is an interface in Selenium WebDriver that provides a way to execute JavaScript code within the context of the browser window

JavaScriptexecutor provides two methods:

`ExecuteScript`
`ExecutrAsyncScript`

Syntax:

```java
JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;
js.executeScript("window.scrollTo(0, 500);");

```

`window.scrollTo(x, y)` is a JavaScript method that can be used to scroll the window to a specified position. In this method, x and y are the coordinates to which the window should be scrolled.

`x` is the horizontal coordinate (the number of pixels to be scrolled to the right from the leftmost edge).

`y` is the vertical coordinate (the number of pixels to be scrolled down from the topmost edge).

`window.scrollTo(0, 500);` means that the window should be scrolled 0 pixels to the right and 500 pixels down from the top of the page.

Let's write hover actions code for [this](https://qadraganddrop.ccbp.tech/) application.

####Scroll down to the page’s bottom using Selenium

Code:

```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;


public class ScrollExample {
    public static void main(String[] args){

        // Set the ChromeDriver location
        System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "path_to_chromedriver");

        // Create a WebDriver instance
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Navigate to the website
        driver.get("https://qadraganddrop.ccbp.tech/");

        // Maximize the browser window
        driver.manage().window().maximize();

        JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;

        js.executeScript("window.scrollTo(0, document.body.scrollHeight)");

        // Close the browser
        driver.quit();

    }
}
```

`document`: This is a global object in JavaScript that represents the entire HTML document. It provides a way to access and manipulate the content, structure, and styles of the web page.

`body`: This property of the document object represents the body element of the web page. The <body> element contains all the visible content of the page, such as text, images, forms, etc.

`scrollHeight`: It is a property of the body object (which represents the body element). It represents the entire height of the content of the element, including the parts that are not currently visible due to scrolling. It's given in pixels


####Scroll to the page’s top using Selenium

To demonstrate this, we first need to scroll down to the page using selenium and then scroll up to the page.

Code:

```java
